class A: def m(self): return self.m() causes infinite recursion, raising RecursionError. The method calls itself forever with no stopping condition.

code:
class A:
    def m(self):
        return self.m()

A().m()

real example:
Infinite self-calls are a bug; real recursion always needs a base case to stop.

Yes, two different classes can define methods with the same name. Methods belong to their own class, so identical names do not clash.

code:
class A:
    def m(self):
        return 1

class B:
    def m(self):
        return 2

real example:
Different payment providers each implement charge() their own way.

print(A().m(), B().m()) outputs 1 2. Each class runs its own method for its own instances.

code:
class A:
    def m(self):
        return 1

class B:
    def m(self):
        return 2

print(A().m(), B().m())

real example:
Animal subclasses each make their own sound through the same method name.

class A: def f(self, *a): return sum(a). print(A().f(1,2,3)) outputs 6. *a collects all positional values into a tuple, then sum adds them.

code:
class A:
    def f(self, *a):
        return sum(a)

print(A().f(1, 2, 3))

real example:
A shopping total method accepts any number of item prices.

object.__new__ allocates and returns the new instance before __init__ runs. It is the first step of object creation.

code:
class A:
    def __new__(cls):
        return super().__new__(cls)

a = A()

real example:
Singletons and immutable types are controlled inside __new__.

__new__ runs first to create the instance, then __init__ runs to initialize it. That ordering is how every object is built.

code:
class A:
    def __new__(cls):
        print('new')
        return super().__new__(cls)

    def __init__(self):
        print('init')

A()

real example:
The creation order matters when subclassing immutable types like tuple.
